    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Diodotus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, seceded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> around the middle of the 3rd century BCE; the exact date is unsettled, between a high chronology of c. 255 BCE and a low one of c. 246 BCE.</li><li>Under Demetrius, son of Euthydemus, the Bactrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> invaded north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between 190 and 180 BCE, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom</a>.</li><li>The Bactrian language continued to be written in the Greek script until the 9th century CE, nearly a thousand years after the kingdom fell.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Greco-Bactrians were the first in the world to issue coins of cupro-nickel, an alloy at the time known only to the Chinese; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-nickel was not used again in coinage until the 19th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

