    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodotus-i.jpg" alt="Diodotus I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodotus_I_of_Bactria_wearing_the_diadem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> long before the kingdom. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> most of the people of Barca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> were deported there, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> the descendants of Greek priests from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a> were forcibly relocated to the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> in 329 BCE and conquered Bactria, placing garrisons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a>, Maracanda and Cyropolis and settling Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in new colonies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> and the Jaxartes; he also massacred the Greek community settled there before him. After Alexander's death Bactria passed to his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>.</p><p>Diodotus, the Seleucid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Bactria, seceded and became king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a>. The ancient sources conflict: a high chronology puts his secession about 255 BCE, a low one in the mid-240s BC, connecting it with the Third Syrian War. Justin calls him the governor of the thousand cities of Bactria. When Arsaces founded the Parthian state, Bactria was cut off from the Greek world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-ii">Diodotus II</a>, his son, allied with Arsaces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus">Seleucus II</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum.jpg" alt="Ai-Khanoum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AiKhanumCapitals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artacoana, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was highly urbanised and, to Justin, one of the richest of the East, the extremely prosperous empire of the thousand cities. Only a few of its cities have been excavated, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> (modern Balkh). Ai-Khanoum had a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> and houses with colonnaded courtyards, Corinthian columns and fragments of a statue 5–6 metres tall; it was burnt around 145 BCE and never resettled. Takht-i Sangin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> held a large temple to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>. Other cities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, Eucratideia and an Amphipolis named after the Macedonian city.</p><p>Military colonists held allotments of land called kleros, and once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> cut the overland routes, sea trade with Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> developed. Around 126 BCE the Han envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> found a population of 1,000,000 or more, people who farmed, lived in cities and were clever at commerce, with a great market at the capital, Lanshi (Bactra), where he saw bamboo from Qiong and cloth from Shu bought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His report led the Han to send envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and to the development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Greco-Bactrian Kingdom&#8221; cites where it covers Greek culture in Bactria, Contacts with Han China:</p><ul><li>F. L. Holt, Alexander the Great and Bactria: The Formation of a Greek Frontier in Central Asia: 2nd Edition (Brill, 1989)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great.jpg" alt="Antiochus III the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_head_wearing_a_head-band_resembling_king_of_Syria_Antiochus_III_(223%E2%80%93187_BC),_late_1st_century_BC%E2%80%93early_1st_century_AD,_Louvre_Museum_(7462828632).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bactrian cavalry had long been famous: 9,000 Bactrian horsemen fought for Darius at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a>, and Alexander added Bactrians to his companion cavalry. Greek garrisons were housed in fortresses called phrouria and in the major cities, and military colonists numbering in the tens of thousands were trained as a Macedonian army. The kingdom's army was multi-ethnic: Greek colonists formed pike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a>, supported by local Bactrian light infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> thureophoroi; a very large cavalry of Bactrian, Sogdian and other horsemen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> and small elite companion units; and Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, shown on coins with towers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>.</p><p>Around 210 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> attacked Euthydemus, who commanded 10,000 horsemen, lost a battle on the Arius and then held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> through a three-year siege until Antiochus recognised him. Demetrius completed the invasion of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by 175 BCE. Eucratides is said to have broken a siege by Demetrius, king of the Indians, who came with 60,000 men, but he was later repulsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a> and defeated by the Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucratides-i.jpg" alt="Eucratides I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eucratides_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eucratides I / BnF, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was ruled by three Greek dynasties. The first dynasty began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a>, who took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-ii">Diodotus II</a>. Euthydemus, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> from Magnesia according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and possibly satrap of Sogdiana, overthrew Diodotus II around 230–220 BCE; his rule reached into Sogdiana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>. An inscription from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulob">Kulob</a> area, dated 200–195 BCE, calls Euthydemus the greatest of all kings and his son Demetrius I Demetrios Kallinikos, the Glorious Conqueror.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a>, either a general of Demetrius or an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, overthrew the Euthydemids around 170 BCE, probably dethroning Antimachus I and Antimachus II. Several kings often ruled at once in different regions, which are not well known. Eucratides struck coins at many Indian mints, possibly as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, and associated a son with his rule. The kings displayed themselves on coins with individual portraits and distinct headgear, such as the kausia, and from Pantaleon onward issued bilingual coins of the Indian standard.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Greco-Bactrians kept contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with which they exchanged ambassadors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> may have been one of the primary channels of Western artistic influence into India, in the Pillars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> capital. Agathocles (ruled 190–180 BCE) issued square coins bearing the first known representations of Indian deities; six were found at Ai-Khanoum in 1970. Their successors in India, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a>, spread Greek art that led to the Greco-Buddhist art of Gandhara.</p><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a> the Greco-Bactrians may have led expeditions as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and Ürümqi, the first known contacts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the West around 220 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> wrote that they extended their empire as far as the Seres. Their coins rank among the finest Greek numismatic art, including the largest gold coin of the Hellenistic world, minted by Eucratides. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> who followed them adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> for their language and Greek gods for their pantheon, and the Bactrian language was written in Greek script until the 9th century CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuezhi.jpg" alt="Yuezhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalchayan_Prince,_side_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eucratides was killed around 145 BCE by his son and joint king, either Eucratides II or Heliocles I, who by Justin's account drove his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> over his father's body. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> west of the Arius, Tapuria and Traxiane, and in 141 BCE the Greco-Bactrians joined the Seleucid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, without lasting success. Heliocles I ruled what remained, weakened and open to nomadic invasion.</p><p>Pushed west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> reached the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>, and around 140 BCE the Saka began to invade Parthia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> visited around 126 BCE he found the Yuezhi holding Bactria under their sway and the country without a great ruler, only petty chiefs ruling its cities. Around 120 BCE the Yuezhi expanded into Bactria, and Heliocles abandoned it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley, making him technically the last Greco-Bactrian king. Greek populations persisted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kings ruled south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> until around 10 CE. The Yuezhi later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Greco-Bactrian Kingdom was a Hellenistic Greek state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, founded when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a> broke from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> in the mid-3rd century BCE. Under the Euthydemid and Eucratid dynasties it ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdiana, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and struck celebrated coins, until Parthian defeats and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> ended it around 120 BCE.</em></p>

